C = [
("h1", "3. Signs That Confirm a Messenger"),
("p", "Claim: in the Old Testament the signs that are miracles are given to a man whom the LORD has sent, and they are given before the people to whom he is sent. Moses, Gideon and Elijah receive them. The text states the purpose each time in the words of the passage: that the people may believe, that the man may be sure, that Israel may know who is God. The volume reads the three accounts in order."),
("sub", "3.1 Moses and the rod"),
("p", "Moses objects at the bush that the people will not accept him: “But, behold, they will not believe me, nor hearken unto my voice: for they will say, The LORD hath not appeared unto thee” (Exodus 4:1). The objection is about credentials, and the LORD answers it with signs. The rod in his hand becomes a serpent and returns to a rod. The hand put into the bosom comes out leprous and is restored. A third sign is held in reserve, the water of the river turned to blood (Exodus 4:2–9)."),
("p", "The purpose is stated twice in the passage. The first is: “That they may believe that the LORD God of their fathers, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob, hath appeared unto thee” (Exodus 4:5). The second is stated for the case of unbelief: “And it shall come to pass, if they will not believe thee, neither hearken to the voice of the first sign, that they will believe the voice of the latter sign” (Exodus 4:8). The sign has a “voice.” The sign is addressed to the elders of Israel, and the LORD arranges the signs in a sequence so that a second can answer the refusal of the first."),
("p", "The passage also names the instrument: “And thou shalt take this rod in thine hand, wherewith thou shalt do signs” (Exodus 4:17). Moses does the signs. They are done by his hand at the LORD’s word, and they are done for the people, not before Pharaoh in the first instance. When Moses and Aaron come to Israel the text records the result: “And Moses told Aaron all the words of the LORD who had sent him, and all the signs which he had commanded him. … And Aaron spake all the words which the LORD had spoken unto Moses, and did the signs in the sight of the people. And the people believed: and when they heard that the LORD had visited the children of Israel, and that he had looked upon their affliction, then they bowed their heads and worshipped” (Exodus 4:28, 30, 31). The order is the words, then the signs, then belief."),
("p", "At the end of the Pentateuch the same life is summed up in the same words: “And there arose not a prophet since in Israel like unto Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face, in all the signs and the wonders, which the LORD sent him to do in the land of Egypt to Pharaoh, and to all his servants, and to all his land, and in all that mighty hand, and in all the great terror which Moses shewed in the sight of all Israel” (Deuteronomy 34:10–12). The signs are listed as the mark of the prophet, with the face-to-face knowledge of the LORD. They are “in the sight of all Israel.”"),
("sub", "3.2 Gideon and the fleece"),
("p", "Gideon asks the angel of the LORD, “If now I have found grace in thy sight, then shew me a sign that thou talkest with me” (Judges 6:17). The request is for confirmation that the speaker is who he says he is. Later Gideon puts out a fleece of wool on the threshing floor and says, “If thou wilt save Israel by mine hand, as thou hast said, … then shall I know that thou wilt save Israel by mine hand” (Judges 6:36, 37). The fleece is wet and the ground dry. He asks again, with the order reversed, and the text records, “And God did so that night: for it was dry upon the fleece only, and there was dew on all the ground” (Judges 6:40)."),
("p", "The account reports what Gideon asked and what God did. It does not call the fleece a command or a pattern. It states the purpose Gideon himself gave, that he might know, and it states the work, that God would save Israel by his hand. The sign is again tied to a messenger whom the LORD is sending to Israel."),
("sub", "3.3 Elijah and the fire"),
("p", "At Carmel Elijah prays: “LORD God of Abraham, Isaac, and of Israel, let it be known this day that thou art God in Israel, and that I am thy servant, and that I have done all these things at thy word. Hear me, O LORD, hear me, that this people may know that thou art the LORD God, and that thou hast turned their heart back again” (1 Kings 18:36, 37). The prayer states the purpose before the sign comes. It asks that Israel know three things: that the LORD is God in Israel, that Elijah is His servant, and that Elijah has acted at His word."),
("p", "The answer is stated in the next verses: “Then the fire of the LORD fell, and consumed the burnt sacrifice, and the wood, and the stones, and the dust, and licked up the water that was in the trench. And when all the people saw it, they fell on their faces: and they said, The LORD, he is the God; the LORD, he is the God” (1 Kings 18:38, 39). The people confess. The sign does what the prayer asked, and it does it before “all the people.”"),
("sub", "3.4 What the three accounts share"),
("p", "In each account the sign is given to a man the LORD has sent, in the sight of the people to whom he is sent, for a purpose the text states. In each the people are Israel. The signs are not performed for display, and they are not performed apart from the message: Moses tells Aaron “all the words of the LORD” before the signs are done; Elijah says he has acted “at thy word”; Gideon’s signs are tied to the saving of Israel that the angel has announced."),
("ctbl", "The messenger signs of the Old Testament.", [
    ("Passage", "The messenger and the sign", "What the text says it is for"),
    ("Exodus 4:1–9", "Moses; the rod, the hand, the water", "“That they may believe that the LORD God of their fathers … hath appeared unto thee”"),
    ("Exodus 4:28–31", "Moses and Aaron; the signs before the people", "The people believed and worshipped"),
    ("Deuteronomy 34:10–12", "Moses; all the signs and wonders", "The mark of a prophet known face to face"),
    ("Judges 6:17, 36–40", "Gideon; the sign and the fleece", "That Gideon may know the LORD will save Israel by his hand"),
    ("1 Kings 18:36–39", "Elijah; the fire at Carmel", "“That this people may know that thou art the LORD God”"),
  ], [0.25, 0.37, 0.38]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The messenger signs of the Old Testament are given to a man the LORD sends, before the people he is sent to.",
    "The purpose is stated each time: belief, knowledge of the LORD, assurance of the word spoken.",
    "The words come first, the signs second, and the signs are tied to the words.",
    "In every account the people are Israel.",
  ]),
]
